Key requirements for `-r`:
1. Directory sources now copy their descendants and empty directories.
2. A missing destination becomes the tree root.
3. An existing directory destination receives source-basename, merging existing directories there.
4. New directories receive source rwx bits masked by umask; existing directories keep their bits.
5. In recursive mode all source symlinks (including operand symlinks and dangling/cyclic links) are recreated with identical target bytes and are not traversed.
6. Without recursive mode preserve earlier symlink behavior (follow source symlinks to regular files).
7. Destination symlink collisions within a copied tree are excluded.
8. Preserve force and interactive behavior for regular files.
9. Reject incompatible types and copying a directory into itself or a descendant.
10. An initial empty output directory may remain, but no descendants may be copied into it.
11. Resource limits must produce a diagnostic rather than looping or crashing.
12. Traversal order is not prescribed.

